+---
+name: creative-feedback-loop
+description: >
+  Two-step pipeline to extract Zilla's creative taste from session logs.
+  Step 1 (fast, no LLM): scan CC + Pi JSONL for ALL assistant→user pairs.
+  No regex, no word lists — just structural pair extraction. Step 2 (parallel
+  Haiku subagents via Claude Agent SDK): classify each pair for sentiment,
+  taste signal, and creative domain. Uses Claude Code's own auth — no API key
+  needed. Outputs a preference index that creative skills can query before
+  generating.
+---
+
+# Creative Feedback Loop
+
+Mine session logs for creative taste signals using Haiku subagents.
+No regex sentiment — every pair gets real LLM judgment.
+
+## When to use this
+
+- Before a creative session — warm up with recent taste signals
+- After a batch of sessions — rebuild the preference index
+- When output feels generic — check what Zilla actually liked/disliked
+- Debugging taste drift in autonomous sessions
+
+## Quick run
+
+### Full pipeline (extract + classify + summary)
+
+```bash
+python3 scripts/extract_pairs.py -o /tmp/creative-pairs.json
+python3 scripts/classify_pairs.py /tmp/creative-pairs.json --summary
+```
+
+### Filter to one project, last 14 days
+
+```bash
+python3 scripts/extract_pairs.py -p wibandwob-dos --recent 14 -o /tmp/pairs.json
+python3 scripts/classify_pairs.py /tmp/pairs.json --summary -o /tmp/taste-index.json
+```
+
+### Pipe mode (no intermediate file)
+
+```bash
+python3 scripts/extract_pairs.py -p wibandwob-dos --recent 7 | \
+  python3 scripts/classify_pairs.py --summary
+```
+
+## How it works
+
+### Step 1: extract_pairs.py (fast, no API calls, no word lists)
+
+Scans `~/.claude/projects/` (CC) and `~/.pi/agent/sessions/` (Pi) for
+ALL adjacent assistant→user message pairs. No regex filtering, no
+heuristic word arrays. Only structural filters:
+- Must be assistant followed by user
+- User message >8 chars (skip single-word acks)
+- Skip hook/system messages
+
+All creative vs engineering judgment is deferred to Haiku in step 2.
+
+Handles both JSONL formats transparently:
+- **CC:** `{type: "user"|"assistant", message: {content}}`
+- **Pi:** `{type: "message", message: {role, content}}`
+
+Output: JSON array of `{assistant_text, user_text, session_id, date, source, project}`
+
+### Step 2: classify_pairs.py (parallel Haiku calls)
+
+Sends each candidate pair to Claude Haiku with a classification prompt.
+Haiku returns structured judgment:
+
+```json
+{
+  "is_creative_reaction": true,
+  "sentiment": "redirect",
+  "taste_signal": "prefers asymmetric layouts, dislikes compressed arrangements",
+  "creative_domain": "layout",
+  "confidence": 0.85
+}
+```
+
+Key advantage over regex: Haiku understands that "the glitch effect is
+cooooool but the first cluster of vox is a bit much" is a REDIRECT, not
+positive. It also filters out non-reactions (engineering follow-ups,
+debugging, hook messages) that regex misclassifies.
+
+Uses Claude Agent SDK `query()` — shares Claude Code's subscription auth,
+no API key needed. Runs 10 concurrent queries by default.
+
+### Sentiment categories
+
+- **positive** — human liked it, wants more of this direction
+- **negative** — human disliked it, wants less of this
+- **redirect** — partially positive but wants specific changes
+- **neutral** — filtered out (acknowledged but no taste signal)
+- **not_a_reaction** — filtered out (engineering, debugging, etc.)
+
+### Taste signals
+
+The most valuable output. Not just positive/negative but WHY:
+- "prefers diagonal cascade layouts over grids"
+- "dislikes overly happy/cheesy chiptune, wants more variety and dynamics"
+- "liked the contour_map + orbit combo, wants more mixed generative types"
+- "concrete poetry arrangements with asymmetric figlet felt right"
+
+## Integration with creative skills
+
+Before generating, include recent taste signals in your prompt:
+
+```
+Recent taste signals (from creative-feedback-loop):
+[+] prefers asymmetric layouts over grid arrangements
+[+] diagonal cascade with mixed window sizes felt right
+[-] too-happy chiptune without dynamics
+[~] liked the glitch effect but vox cluster was too dense
+```
+
+## Options
+
+### extract_pairs.py
+
+```
+--cc-dir DIR       CC projects dir (default: ~/.claude/projects)
+--pi-dir DIR       Pi sessions dir (default: ~/.pi/agent/sessions)
+--project, -p STR  Filter to project slug (substring match)
+--recent N         Only process sessions from last N days
+--output, -o FILE  Output JSON file (default: stdout)
+```
+
+### classify_pairs.py
+
+```
+input              JSON file from extract_pairs (or - for stdin)
+--output, -o FILE  Output classified JSON file
+--summary          Print human-readable taste report
+--concurrency, -c  Max parallel Haiku calls (default: 15)
+--model MODEL      Classification model (default: claude-haiku-4-5-20251001)
+--min-confidence F Minimum confidence threshold (default: 0.4)
+```
+
+## Auth & Cost
+
+Uses `claude-agent-sdk` `query()` function — inherits Claude Code's
+subscription auth. No ANTHROPIC_API_KEY needed.
+
+Haiku classification: ~200 input tokens + ~80 output tokens per pair.
+Cheap enough to run on every session.
+
+## Files
+
+```
+scripts/
+  extract_pairs.py    # Step 1: JSONL -> candidate pairs (fast, no LLM)
+  classify_pairs.py   # Step 2: Haiku classification (parallel subagents)
+```
+
+## Future
+
+- Auto-run step 1 on session end (hook), batch classify nightly
+- Embedding similarity: "find taste signals for things LIKE this output"
+- Per-domain profiles: separate art/music/layout/typography taste
+- Conductor agent integration: taste index as generation constraint
+- Conductor self-assessment reactions flagged as source="conductor"
